# What NOT to upload to Vercel for the API project (root directory = repo root).
#
# Only list paths that are ABSENT from the machine that runs `vercel build`.
#
# CI deploys with `vercel build` + `vercel deploy --prebuilt`. The build bundles every
# file it can reach into .vercel/output's file map -- Python functions have no
# tree-shaking -- and the upload then applies this file. Excluding something that the
# build already recorded leaves the deployment referencing a file that was never sent:
#
#   Error: ENOENT: no such file or directory, lstat '/vercel/path0/.github/workflows/ci.yml'
#
# So nothing tracked in git belongs here: a CI checkout has all of it. Everything below
# is either gitignored or generated, which is why excluding it is safe.
#
# The point of this file is the first entry. Vercel uploads the working directory rather
# than the git tree, so .gitignore does not protect a local .env -- and pydantic-settings
# reads env_file=".env" at runtime, meaning any variable not set on the Vercel project
# would silently fall back to a developer's local value, including an AI provider and its
# key. CI never has one; a deploy run from a laptop would.

# Listed one by one rather than as .env.* with negations for the examples: those two
# ARE tracked, so if the negation did not apply they would be excluded, bundled, and
# then missing -- the same ENOENT described above.
.env
.env.local
.env.development.local
.env.preview.local
.env.production.local

.venv/
node_modules/
.next/

__pycache__/
*.pyc
.pytest_cache/
.ruff_cache/
.mypy_cache/

# Gitignored working data, not part of the application.
references/
.claude/
